Hm. Idea:

 - You should not be able to #twoweapon at all with a weapon you are
"Unskilled" in, no matter how good your #twoweapon skill. ("You aren't
skilled enough with that weapon to dual-wield it.")

 - If hitting a monster while #twoweaponing:
     (1) If your skill in EITHER weapon is *equal to or lesser than*
your current #twoweapon skill, then you do not exercise your
#twoweapon skill at all. Instead, you exercise your skill in the
weapon you hit with.
     (2) Whereas, if your skill in BOTH weapons is *greater than* your
current #twoweapon skill, you exercise your #twoweapon skill when
hitting with either, and do not exercise your weapon skill.

So, when dual-wielding:

 - if one or both of your weapons is at Basic skill, you cannot
improve your #twoweapon beyond Basic, but if you continue
#twoweaponing then eventually you will improve both weapons to Skilled
(if they can reach that category), and when they BOTH reach Skilled
(one may have reached Expert by now, then again it may not), then you
stop improving in them and start improving in #twoweaponing instead,
from Basic to Skilled. Of course if you're restricted to Basic in one
of the weapons, you cannot exercise your #twoweapon skill at all.

 - if both your weapons are Skilled or better, then you can improve
your #twoweapon skill to Skilled, and after that you stop improving
your #twoweapon when you strike, but instead start improving the
skills of the individual weapon that actually hits the monster -
assuming you CAN take that weapon to Expert at all.

 - but in order to get train up to Expert at #twoweaponing, you must
ALREADY be Expert in both weapons that you are wielding (if they're
the same type, of course, then you save yourself a lot of bother, e.g.
two longswords.)

